-- Macaulay2 sketch of the First Law of Modular Thermodynamics / First Law of Entanglement Entropy.
-- We illustrate the identity:
--   dS = d⟨K⟩
-- where S is the von Neumann entropy, K is the modular Hamiltonian, and ⟨·⟩ denotes expectation value.
--
-- This is done by constructing algebraic representations of the relevant quantities
-- and showing how the differential relationship emerges from the definitions.

-- Define a ring to work over (we'll use QQ for simplicity, though in practice 
-- one might want to work over more complex rings depending on the context)
R = QQ[s, k, q];  -- s for entropy, k for modular Hamiltonian expectation, q for partition function

-- Define the entropy in terms of expectation value and partition function
-- S = ⟨K⟩ + ln Q
-- Note: In Macaulay2, we don't have direct log function in the polynomial ring,
-- so we'll work symbolically or note that we'd need to extend to a suitable function field

-- Instead, let's work with the exponential form which is more algebraic
-- From S = ⟨K⟩ + ln Q, we get e^S = e^{⟨K⟩} * Q

-- Define expressions
expS = exp(s);  -- This would require working in a suitable extension
expK = exp(k);

-- The relationship: expS = expK * q
-- Or equivalently: expS - expK*q = 0

idealI = ideal(expr_s - expr_k*q);  -- This is pseudocode; actual implementation would need more care

-- For the differential version, we would consider the differential ring or jet space
-- dS = d⟨K⟩ + d(ln Q)
-- For normalized states, Q = 1, so d(ln Q) = 0, hence dS = d⟨K⟩

-- In Macaulay2, we could demonstrate this by working in the ring of differential operators
-- or by showing that the relation holds in the appropriate quotient

-- Example verification in a simple case:
-- Suppose we parameterize by a variable t, and we want to show that 
-- d/dt S = d/dt ⟨K⟩ when Q=1

-- Let's define a parameterized version
Rt = QQ[t];
s_t = t^2;  -- example entropy function
k_t = t^2;  -- example expectation value (would be equal in this case)
q_t = 1;    -- partition function for normalized state

-- Check that S = k + ln(q) holds
-- Since q_t = 1, ln(q_t) = 0, so we need s_t = k_t
-- In our example, s_t = t^2 and k_t = t^2, so s_t = k_t holds

-- Then d/dt s_t = 2t and d/dt k_t = 2t, so d/dt s_t = d/dt k_t

-- Print the relationship
print("First Law of Modular Thermodynamics in Algebraic Form");
print("==================================================");
print("");
print("Definitions:");
print("  Let S be the von Neumann entropy");
print("  Let ⟨K⟩ be the expectation value of the modular Hamiltonian");
print("  Let Q = Tr(e^{-K}) be the partition function");
print("");
print("Fundamental relation: S = ⟨K⟩ + ln Q");
print("");
print("For normalized states (Tr(ρ) = 1):");
print("  We have Q = 1, hence ln Q = 0");
print("  Therefore S = ⟨K⟩");
print("");
print("Taking differentials:");
print("  dS = d⟨K⟩ + d(ln Q)");
print("");
print("For normalized states, d(ln Q) = 0, so:");
print("  dS = d⟨K⟩");
print("");
print("This is the First Law of Modular Thermodynamics:");
print("  The change in entropy equals the change in the");
print("  expectation value of the modular Hamiltonian.");
print("");
print("In the context of algebraic geometry and commutative algebra,");
print("this relationship can be studied in:");
print("  - Rings of functions on state spaces");
print("  - Differential algebras or jet spaces");
print("  - Modules of differentials (Kähler differentials)");
print("");
print("For example, if we have a family of states parameterized by t,");
print("and we form the ideal generated by S - ⟨K⟩ - ln(Q),");
print("then showing that dS - d⟨K⟩ lies in the appropriate differential");
print("ideal would establish the first law formally.");